Negative control for feature-tracks.spec.ts (T4.1, AC2.5 — the input to design
§3.1 ruling 2).

A track's DECLARED SCOPE is what the player compares against the page's current
scope before it arms, and a mismatch is what sends the page to that scope FIRST
(design §3.1 ruling 2 / §7.1). The client-emails page's 11th scenario is the only one that
declares a different scope, and it declares it the one way a Gherkin scenario
can: its arrangement boots at it. So the one mechanism carrying that fact is the
scope probe replaying the LEADING GIVEN scenes against a recording world.

This mutant reads the wrong arrangement: it probes the leading `When` scenes,
of which every driven scenario has none in front, so nothing boots against the
probe and every track comes back with no declared scope at all. Nothing throws,
the playlist still parses into 11 playable tracks, and the staff track now looks
like an in-place one — which is precisely the invisible failure §7.1 names, since
a player with no mismatch to see would arm a foreign scope on the page's own
cell.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: feature-tracks.spec.ts — the declared-scope case (the staff track carries
the scope its arrangement boots at, the client tracks carry theirs).

